    {
      "id": "Xwd5EX0iwJyEP0hsCPxp3",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 13875.757333181973,
      "y": 15355.642868976272,
      "width": 911.4674595424095,
      "height": 325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "5CZbdNRyhlHPkTZYsbxs-"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b8ZG",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1204752535,
      "version": 239,
      "versionNonce": 12299737,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "7CoTirFetXC4yb5ttVmA3",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1754109714435,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "ReLU Activation Function\n\nWhat’s happening: A ReLU (Rectified Linear Unit) activation function is defined.\nWhy:\n\nReLU applies the function f(x) = max(0, x) to each element in the input, introducing non-\nlinearity to the model.\nNon-linearities allow neural networks to learn complex patterns. Without them, stacking linear\nlayers (like fully connected layers) would behave like a single linear transformation.\n\n\nKey point: ReLU is computationally efficient and helps mitigate the vanishing gradient problem\ncompared to older activations like sigmoid.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "ReLU Activation Function\n\nWhat’s happening: A ReLU (Rectified Linear Unit) activation function is defined.\nWhy:\n\nReLU applies the function f(x) = max(0, x) to each element in the input, introducing non-linearity to the model.\nNon-linearities allow neural networks to learn complex patterns. Without them, stacking linear layers (like fully connected layers) would behave like a single linear transformation.\n\n\nKey point: ReLU is computationally efficient and helps mitigate the vanishing gradient problem compared to older activations like sigmoid.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
