        "5CZbdNRyhlHPkTZYsbxs-"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b8ZS",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1473893815,
      "version": 249,
      "versionNonce": 322798809,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "m7IlVtNyZhZDVkz40MM9R",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1754109714435,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Second Fully Connected (Output) Layer\n\nWhat’s happening: Another fully connected layer takes the 512-dimensional output from the previous layer and maps\nit to 2 dimensions.\nWhy:\n\nThe output size of 2 corresponds to the number of classes in the classification task (e.g., positive/negative for\nbinary classification).\nThis layer produces logits (raw scores) for each class, which will later be converted to probabilities.\n\n\nKey point: The choice of 2 as the output size indicates this model is designed for binary classification. For multi-\nclass tasks, the output size would match the number of classes.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Second Fully Connected (Output) Layer\n\nWhat’s happening: Another fully connected layer takes the 512-dimensional output from the previous layer and maps it to 2 dimensions.\nWhy:\n\nThe output size of 2 corresponds to the number of classes in the classification task (e.g., positive/negative for binary classification).\nThis layer produces logits (raw scores) for each class, which will later be converted to probabilities.\n\n\nKey point: The choice of 2 as the output size indicates this model is designed for binary classification. For multi-class tasks, the output size would match the number of classes.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
