        }
      ],
      "updated": 1754109714435,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "LogSoftmax Activation Function\n\nWhat’s happening: A LogSoftmax function is defined, applied along dimension 1.\nWhy:\n\nSoftmax converts logits into probabilities that sum to 1, making it suitable for classification tasks.\nLogSoftmax applies the logarithm to the Softmax output, which is numerically stable when used with certain loss functions\n(like nn.NLLLoss, Negative Log Likelihood Loss).\ndim=1 specifies that Softmax is applied across the class dimension (the 2 outputs), not the batch dimension.\n\n\nKey point: LogSoftmax is often paired with NLLLoss for classification tasks, as it simplifies the loss computation and\navoids numerical instability.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "LogSoftmax Activation Function\n\nWhat’s happening: A LogSoftmax function is defined, applied along dimension 1.\nWhy:\n\nSoftmax converts logits into probabilities that sum to 1, making it suitable for classification tasks.\nLogSoftmax applies the logarithm to the Softmax output, which is numerically stable when used with certain loss functions (like nn.NLLLoss, Negative Log Likelihood Loss).\ndim=1 specifies that Softmax is applied across the class dimension (the 2 outputs), not the batch dimension.\n\n\nKey point: LogSoftmax is often paired with NLLLoss for classification tasks, as it simplifies the loss computation and avoids numerical instability.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
